<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Priene</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Priene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Ionian city near Mount Mycale, home of the sage Bias and later rebuilt as the best-preserved example of a Hellenistic grid-planned town.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Priene was one of the twelve Ionian cities, founded by tradition by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> c. 1000 BCE. Its original site near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> has never been found. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Panionion</a>, the common sanctuary of the Ionian League on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>, lay in its territory, giving Priene a role in Ionian affairs out of proportion to its size.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 6th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bias-of-priene">Bias of Priene</a>, counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece">Seven Sages</a>, advised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> to emigrate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> rather than submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Priene fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, sent 12 ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE and later paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the mid-4th century BCE the city was rebuilt on a terrace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mycale</a> with a strict grid; Alexander dedicated its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena-polias">Athena Polias</a>. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> silted its harbour, Priene declined, leaving its ruins remarkably intact.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/priene.jpg" alt="Priene" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Ken and Nyetta · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Athena_at_Priene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Priene</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Athena Polias</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=priene" aria-label="Priene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5362 823 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Priene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5682 1079L6140 1251M5682 1079L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 1079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 1079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=priene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

